{"type":"message","id":"90f9202b","parentId":"c95206ce","timestamp":"2026-10-17T22:46:27.832Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed `parse_date` in `dates/parse.py`. It now uses `datetime.fromisoformat(s)` instead of `strptime(s, \"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S\")`, which had no way to read the trailing `Z`. All 3 tests in `tests/test_parse.py` pass.\n\nWhat it returns now:\n- A timestamp ending in `Z` comes back with its timezone set to UTC (`tzinfo=timezone.utc`).\n- A timestamp without a zone, like `\"2026-01-02T03:04:05\"`, still comes back with no timezone, so `test_naive` and `day_key` behave as before.\n- Offsets like `+02:00` now work too.\n\nTwo things to be aware of:\n- **Python version:** reading `Z` with `fromisoformat` needs Python 3.11 or later. This environment has 3.13, and the repo doesn't pin a version. If you need older Python, the fallback is `strptime` with `%z`, used only when the string has a zone.\n- **Mixed results:** calls now return a mix of zone-aware and zone-less datetimes. Python raises an error if you compare or subtract one kind from the other, so any code that does that with these results will fail."}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"vertex-anthropic","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":402,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":27507,"totalTokens":27911,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cttl":{"ephemeral5m":27507}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1792277183289,"responseId":"msg_vrtx_011Cg8d28wLKB6p4S6VLcKsz","duration":4542.516520000001,"ttft":4514.203077000002,"contextSnapshot":{"promptTokens":27509,"nonMessageTokens":21589,"compactionEpoch":0}}}
